On Monday, April 3, 2023 at 7:38:32 PM UTC+10 Terrence Jorgensen wrote:
> Wu and Estabrook's (2016) introduction discusses the distinction between identification constraints and hypothesized equation constraints.  When you leave variances fixed (an identification constraint in a configural model) after imposing hypothesized equality of loadings, then you are not testing metric invariance.  You are instead simultaneously testing equality of loadings AND factor variances.  Likewise with factor means when you constrain intercepts: the fixed means would no longer be identification constraints when they are not needed for identification.  There are also several threads on this forum you could search for my discussions of invariance in general and Wu and Estabrook (2016) specifically.  Notice also that relative to the configural model, equating thresholds also releases some constraints that are no longer needed for identification (check latent-response intercepts and residual variances per indicator).
