Hi Terrence,

If you don't mind, I have a similar question. I am interested in whether enjoyment mediates the effects of the intervention (group, with 3 levels) on performance. If I add group as the predictor, I should check whether the indirect effect differs from zero. This is easy to understand.
But if I make two dummy variables for the group and add them as the predictors in mediation (e.g., group 0 is the reference group). What should I check and report?

Best,
Yuan
Yuanyuan Hu | PhD Candidate | Department of Education | Utrecht University | Heidelberglaan 1, 3584 CS Utrecht, the Netherlands | Room  E3.13  | 068 514 0270 | y....@uu.nl

在2019年8月17日星期六 UTC+2 15:37:43<Terrence Jorgensen> 写道：
> > I checked lavaan's website about categorical variables <http://lavaan.ugent.be/tutorial/cat.html>, but I don't see any information on what to do for nominal variables
>
> The very top section tells you to make dummy codes for k-1 categories to use as predictors.  You would include both in your syntax, and define indirect effects separately from each of them through the mediator (both interpreted as effects of being in that group, relative to your chosen reference category).
